-- ownership: what mutually recursive types hold is solved once per cycle
-- (#341). Whether a type may hold a list is the least fixpoint over the
-- types its definition names. A first fix of the mutual-recursion hole
-- (`MutualTypes`) remembered only the outermost type of a cycle and read
-- every other one again on each path through it: twelve types that each
-- name all the others took 27 s to check, and twenty-eight that each name
-- the next three 9 s. Each strongly connected group is solved once now.
-- `f` pushes onto the list of its parameter, and `g` onto one reached
-- through the chain: both accepted, `param`.
type K0
    = K0
        (List Int)
        (Maybe K1)
        (Maybe K2)
        (Maybe K3)
        (Maybe K4)
        (Maybe K5)
        (Maybe K6)
        (Maybe K7)
        (Maybe K8)
        (Maybe K9)
        (Maybe K10)
        (Maybe K11)
    | KEnd0


